  {
   "id": "h2-42",
   "name": "Convection: examples, advantages and disadvantages"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Examples of convection in the kitchen and home:\n• boiling water, soups and sauces in a pot;\n• deep frying: hot oil circulates around the food;\n• an oven: hot air rises to the top, so the top shelf is the hottest;\n• smoke and hot air rising from a fire, through a chimney or kitchen roof;\n• a refrigerator: the cold freezer box is at the top, so cold air sinks and cools the food below;\n• ventilation: hot, stale air leaves through high openings and cool air enters below.\nAdvantages: liquids and air are heated evenly; ovens and pots cook food all round; good ventilation removes smoke and heat.\nDisadvantages: heat is lost upwards from uncovered pots (cover pots to save fuel); food on the top shelf of an oven may burn; smoke spreads through a closed kitchen.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "the freezer box is at the top of a refrigerator"
  },
  "problem": "Explain why {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "The air cooled by the freezer box becomes denser and sinks.",
   "It flows down over the food, and warmer air rises to be cooled: a convection current."
  ],
  "answer": "Cold air sinks and cools all the food below.",
  "checks": []
 },
